      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8cb4b0ef-6e4c-58be-9040-1632e5a6912a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Jackson Davis, Francis Key Hunt, Francis Scott Key\n- Places: Loudoun House, Terra Rubra, Frederick, Maryland, Georgetown, D.C., Washington, D.C.\n- Roles: architect, lawyer, author, amateur poet\n- Works: \"The Defence of Fort McHenry\", \"The Star-Spangled Banner\"\n\nFacts:\n- Francis Scott Key was born in 1779 at Terra Rubra.\n- Francis Scott Key was an American lawyer, author, and amateur poet from Frederick, Maryland, and later Georgetown, D.C., near Washington, D.C.\n- Francis Scott Key wrote the lyrics for a poem first titled \"The Defence of Fort McHenry,\" which, when set to an old English gentlemen's society tune, became the United States' national anthem, \"The Star-Spangled Banner.\"\n- Loudoun House was designed by New York architect Alexander Jackson Davis and built in 1851 for Francis Key Hunt.\n- Francis Key Hunt was born in 1817, died in 1879, and was named after his mother's cousin, Francis Scott Key.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
